[{'["# Feature name: rank_mean\\n# Feature description: The mean rank of all the five cards.\\n# Usefulness: This feature provides a normalized representation of the rank distribution. It can be helpful for identifying combinations where rank differences across cards might matter less (e.g., distributions for Target=0, 1, 2). It also works in conjunction with other rank-based features like rank_difference_std to provide further clarity on hand classification.\\n\\ndf[\'rank_mean\'] = df[[\'V2\', \'V4\', \'V6\', \'V8\', \'V10\']].mean(axis=1)", "# Feature name: rank_max_min_diff\\n# Feature description: The difference between the highest and lowest ranks across the five cards.\\n# Usefulness: Highlights the rank spread within a hand. Can be useful for identifying hands like Straight (Target=4) as well as distinguishing certain combinations (e.g., smaller spreads in hands with more rank matches like Full House or Four of a Kind). Serves as a complementary feature to rank_difference_std.\\n\\ndf[\'rank_max_min_diff\'] = df[[\'V2\', \'V4\', \'V6\', \'V8\', \'V10\']].apply(lambda x: max(x) - min(x), axis=1)", "# Feature name: suit_entropy\\n# Feature description: The entropy of suits across the five cards.\\n# Usefulness: Provides a measure of the diversity of suits in the hand. Higher entropy suggests varied suits, making it less likely to be a Flush or Royal Flush. Lower entropy indicates similarity in suits, aiding in classification of these specific hand types (Target=5, 8, 9).\\n\\nfrom scipy.stats import entropy\\ndf[\'suit_entropy\'] = df[[\'V1\', \'V3\', \'V5\', \'V7\', \'V9\']].apply(lambda x: entropy([list(x).count(suit) for suit in set(x)]), axis=1)", "# Feature name: is_low_straight\\n# Feature description: Boolean flag indicating whether the hand is a Low Straight consisting of ranks {Ace, 2, 3, 4, 5}.\\n# Usefulness: Helps specifically identify a variant of Straight (Target=4), accounting for the unique wrap-around rules where Ace can act as 1 for lower straights.\\n\\ndf[\'is_low_straight\'] = df[[\'V2\', \'V4\', \'V6\', \'V8\', \'V10\']].apply(lambda x: set(x) == {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, axis=1)", "# Feature name: flush_strength\\n# Feature description: Encodes the strength of a Flush hand based on the sum of ranks when all suits are identical.\\n# Usefulness: Facilitates differentiation between Flush (Target=5) and stronger hands like Straight Flush (Target=8) or Royal Flush (Target=9). Useful for ranking hands within the same category.\\n\\ndf[\'flush_strength\'] = df.apply(\\n    lambda row: row[\'rank_sum\'] if row[\'is_flush\'] else 0, axis=1\\n)", "# Feature name: rank_median\\n# Feature description: Median of the ranks in the hand.\\n# Usefulness: Provides a central tendency of ranks in the hand for distinguishing between different combinations more effectively. It is particularly helpful in categorizing lower-ranked or higher-ranked hands (e.g., Target=0 or Target=9).\\n\\ndf[\'rank_median\'] = df[[\'V2\', \'V4\', \'V6\', \'V8\', \'V10\']].median(axis=1)", "# Feature name: normalized_rank_sum\\n# Feature description: The rank_sum feature normalized by the total possible sum of ranks (65 for the highest-ranked combination).\\n# Usefulness: Provides a scaled version of rank_sum to compare hands based on their relative rank power. Helps in differentiating Royal Flush (Target=9) with a perfect high score versus weaker combinations or random hands (Target=0).\\n\\ndf[\'normalized_rank_sum\'] = df[\'rank_sum\'] / 65", "df.drop(columns=[\'V1\', \'V3\', \'V5\', \'V7\', \'V9\'], inplace=True)", "df.drop(columns=[\'V2\', \'V4\', \'V6\', \'V8\', \'V10\'], inplace=True)"]': 0.0}, {'["# Feature name: card_suit_match_count\\n# Feature description: The number of suits that are the same across the five cards.\\n# Usefulness: Helps identify hands like Flush (five cards with the same suit) and contributes to detecting Straight Flush and Royal Flush. Additionally, it may help differentiate other combinations like nothing (Target=0).\\