2026-05-13 04:44:39,816 - INFO - ops_string = ["# Feature name: high_card_presence_flag\n# Feature description: Boolean flag indicating whether the hand contains at least one card of rank 10 or higher.\n# Usefulness: Detects the presence of high-value cards such as Ten, Jack, Queen, King, and Ace. These cards are critical for identifying Royal Flush (Target=9), high-ranking Flushes (Target=5), and overall high-value hands. By isolating high-value cards, the model can better differentiate strong hands from weaker ones.\ndf['high_card_presence_flag'] = df.apply(\n    lambda row: any(rank >= 10 for rank in [row['V2'], row['V4'], row['V6'], row['V8'], row['V10']]),\n    axis=1\n)", "# Feature name: suit_entropy\n# Feature description: The entropy of suits across the five cards.\n# Usefulness: Provides a measure of the diversity of suits in the hand. Higher entropy suggests varied suits, making it less likely to be a Flush (Target=5), Straight Flush (Target=8), or Royal Flush (Target=9). Lower entropy indicates similarity in suits, aiding in classification of these specific hand types. Helps reduce false positives for mixed-suit combinations.\nfrom scipy.stats import entropy\ndf['suit_entropy'] = df[['V1', 'V3', 'V5', 'V7', 'V9']].apply(\n    lambda x: entropy([list(x).count(suit) for suit in set(x)]), axis=1\n)", "# Feature name: is_low_straight\n# Feature description: Boolean flag indicating whether the hand is a Low Straight consisting of ranks {Ace, 2, 3, 4, 5}.\n# Usefulness: Helps specifically identify a variant of Straight (Target=4), accounting for the unique wrap-around rules where Ace can act as 1 for lower straights. This ensures the model can detect these edge-case Straights with better accuracy.\ndf['is_low_straight'] = df[['V2', 'V4', 'V6', 'V8', 'V10']].apply(\n    lambda x: set(x) == {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, axis=1\n)", "# Feature name: suit_rank_ratio\n# Feature description: The ratio of card_suit_match_count to card_rank_match_count.\n# Usefulness: Captures the relationship between suit and rank matching, highlighting the interplay between rank-based hands (One Pair, Two Pair, Full House, etc.) and suit-based hands (Flush, Straight Flush, and Royal Flush). Helps the model understand how rank and suit similarities correlate with high-value hands.\ndf['suit_rank_ratio'] = df['card_suit_match_count'] / (df['card_rank_match_count'] + 1e-6)", "# Feature name: rank_std_dev_norm_ratio\n# Feature description: The ratio of rank_difference_std to the normalized rank sum (rank_sum_normalized).\n# Usefulness: Highlights the contrast between the rank distribution variability and the average rank strength per card. This feature provides additional support for detecting patterns in hands like Straights (Target=4) or Straight Flushes (Target=8). It can also help the model fine-tune classification of other sequential patterns.\ndf['rank_std_dev_norm_ratio'] = df['rank_difference_std'] / (df['rank_sum_normalized'] + 1e-6)", "# Feature name: flush_rank_spread\n# Feature description: The difference between the highest and lowest ranks in a hand, calculated only if the hand is a Flush (is_flush=True).\n# Usefulness: Helps refine the classification between simple Flush (Target=5) and stronger hands like Straight Flush (Target=8) or Royal Flush (Target=9). By looking at the spread of ranks, this feature can highlight sequential patterns in rank-heavy Flush combinations.\ndf['flush_rank_spread'] = df.apply(\n    lambda row: max([row['V2'], row['V4'], row['V6'], row['V8'], row['V10']]) - \n                min([row['V2'], row['V4'], row['V6'], row['V8'], row['V10']]) \n                if row['is_flush'] else 0, \n    axis=1\n)", "# Feature name: zscore_rank_sum\n# Feature description: The z-score normalization of the rank_sum feature.\n# Usefulness: Identifies outliers and extreme values in rank distributions, which can highlight hands like Royal Flush (Target=9) or Straight Flush (Target=8). Z-score normalization ensures that rank_sum values are standardized for consistent comparability across the dataset.\ndf['zscore_rank_sum'] = (df['rank_sum'] - df['rank_sum'].mean()) / df['rank_sum'].std()"]
